# Paths in the BASE Puppy layer that must stay group/other-writable (engine#1474). One prefix per line, relative to the
# root of the layered tree; `#` and blank lines are ignored. Everything not listed here has `go-w` applied by
# `pack_normalise_base_modes` -- the group and other WRITE bits only, never any other bit: setting "other files 0644" the
# way the payload's normaliser does would make /etc/shadow world-readable, and this is an operating system's base layer.
# A prefix covers the path itself as well as everything below it, which is why `tmp/` keeps /tmp's 1777.
#
# The list is short on purpose and every entry is a place where the running system WRITES at boot or at runtime. A
# missing entry breaks the appliance in ways a boot finds late, which is why the release row prints this file: a change
# here is visible instead of silent.
tmp/
var/tmp/
var/run/
var/lock/
var/spool/
var/log/
run/
dev/
proc/
sys/
# The payload is NOT this list's business: it has its own normaliser (pack_normalise_modes) and its own release row,
# and since c33 it ships 0 group/other-writable files (engine#1352). Keeping it here means the base row can be read as
# "the base layer outside the payload", which is the number engine#1474 is about.
opt/1bit-package/
